1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,320
At some point a message like this arrives.
A branch is a named line of commits, the

2
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,711
saved snapshots of a project, and main
is the branch the whole team shares.

3
00:00:13,751 --> 00:00:16,990
The CTO who receives the
message doesn't want a command.

4
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:21,732
The CTO wants four answers: what happened,
how you know, what you changed and how

5
00:00:21,772 --> 00:00:24,777
you proved it worked, and
why it won't happen again.

6
00:00:24,817 --> 00:00:29,051
Keep those four answers in view. Every
drill in this part ends with them.

7
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:32,423
You have a method for one
repository and one symptom.

8
00:00:32,463 --> 00:00:35,958
An incident adds three things
the method alone doesn't cover.

9
00:00:35,998 --> 00:00:40,711
Other people, whose clones, their own copies
of the repository, hold evidence, and

10
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:45,464
who keep working while you investigate.
Time pressure, which makes the destructive

11
00:00:45,504 --> 00:00:50,159
shortcut attractive. And an audience that
has to decide something on the basis of

12
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:51,260
what you tell them.

13
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:56,814
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:56,854 --> 00:01:01,490
This video opens the incident drills.
No new Git mechanism is taught in this

15
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:04,997
chapter. Every recovery uses
techniques you already have.

16
00:01:05,037 --> 00:01:10,166
What is new is the order in which you apply
them when the report is wrong, the clock

17
00:01:10,206 --> 00:01:12,504
is running, and three clones disagree.

18
00:01:12,844 --> 00:01:16,996
Three things today. The loop that
handles an incident, in seven stages.

19
00:01:17,036 --> 00:01:21,837
A severity scale and three rules for using
it. And the practical part: how the ten

20
00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:26,324
incidents are built, how you generate
one, enter it and check your recovery.

21
00:01:26,664 --> 00:01:30,161
Then one rule that governs every
remaining video of this part.

22
00:01:30,201 --> 00:01:34,669
Each incident video has a pause point.
You generate the incident and attempt it

23
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:39,234
before you watch the debrief. A drill
whose cause you've seen can't be repeated.

24
00:01:41,074 --> 00:01:42,653
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:42,993 --> 00:01:44,405
After this video you can:

26
00:01:44,745 --> 00:01:47,554
Name the stages of the
incident loop in order.

27
00:01:47,894 --> 00:01:51,467
Rate the severity of a Git or GitHub
incident and defend the rating.

28
00:01:51,807 --> 00:01:55,426
Generate an incident sandbox,
enter it and check a recovery.

29
00:01:55,766 --> 00:01:57,876
Follow the ten-part format for a drill.

30
00:01:58,216 --> 00:02:01,404
Explain why the solution is
read only after an attempt.

31
00:02:03,244 --> 00:02:07,864
An incident, in one sentence. An incident
is a problem in shared state, a shared

32
00:02:07,904 --> 00:02:12,639
branch, a published history, a credential
such as a password or token, a pipeline,

33
00:02:12,679 --> 00:02:16,716
that other people depend on while
you're still working out what it is.

34
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:22,004
The loop. Seven stages. It's the root-cause
framework with the stages that only

35
00:02:22,044 --> 00:02:24,340
exist when other people are involved.

36
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:29,910
Two rules hold the loop together. Stages
2 and 3 use only commands that add refs,

37
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:32,968
the names that point at
commits, or read state.

38
00:02:33,308 --> 00:02:38,400
And stage 6 starts early: a first message
goes out as soon as you know which branch

39
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,863
people must leave alone, long
before you know the cause.

40
00:02:42,203 --> 00:02:47,446
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Cover
the table and write the seven stages in

41
00:02:47,486 --> 00:02:50,630
order. Then draw the arrow
for that first message.

42
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:58,653
Stabilise, preserve, diagnose,
recover, verify, communicate, prevent.

43
00:02:58,693 --> 00:03:02,220
And the arrow runs from
stage 1 to stage 6.

44
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:07,385
Where the evidence lives. In an incident
the evidence is spread over several

45
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:10,842
repositories, and each kind
lives in a specific place.

46
00:03:10,882 --> 00:03:15,452
Much of it is in reflogs, Git's local
records of each ref's past values.

47
00:03:15,792 --> 00:03:21,466
A prediction. Every clone keeps reflogs.
Does the server keep one for its branches?

48
00:03:21,506 --> 00:03:22,568
Say it out loud.

49
00:03:27,408 --> 00:03:32,415
Three of the places are reflogs in each
clone: of each local branch, of HEAD, and of

50
00:03:32,455 --> 00:03:34,098
each remote-tracking branch.

51
00:03:34,438 --> 00:03:39,229
What the server holds now comes from git
ls-remote origin, staged content survives

52
00:03:39,269 --> 00:03:43,353
as a dangling blob, and who pushed
what is GitHub's record, not Git's.

53
00:03:43,693 --> 00:03:48,920
No, not by default. The retention
periods are Git's defaults, and the lab

54
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:54,404
configuration switches reflog expiry
off. And one fact decides how you treat

55
00:03:54,444 --> 00:04:00,464
colleagues' clones: a bare repository, which
is what a server holds, keeps no reflog

56
00:04:00,504 --> 00:04:03,062
unless core.logAllRefUpdates is set.

57
00:04:03,402 --> 00:04:08,510
So the history of a server-side branch
exists only as the sum of what the clones

58
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:11,920
remember, plus whatever the
hosting platform records.

59
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:17,197
That's why "preserve" includes asking
colleagues not to run git fetch --prune, git

60
00:04:17,237 --> 00:04:22,216
gc or a re-clone until you've read their
reflogs. Each of those can delete old

61
00:04:22,256 --> 00:04:22,731
records.

62
00:04:23,071 --> 00:04:27,994
Severity. Severity decides who is told
and how fast, not how interesting the Git

63
00:04:28,034 --> 00:04:33,143
problem is. The textbook's scale is a working
scale for repository incidents, to be

64
00:04:33,183 --> 00:04:37,857
adapted to your organisation's own. It
asks three questions: which state was

65
00:04:37,897 --> 00:04:42,696
affected, could wrong code or a secret
have reached users or outsiders, and is

66
00:04:42,736 --> 00:04:44,123
anything unrecoverable.

67
00:04:44,463 --> 00:04:49,193
Three rules for using a scale. Assign the
level on what could have happened in the

68
00:04:49,233 --> 00:04:53,730
window, not only on what did. Raise the
level the moment a secret is involved.

69
00:04:53,770 --> 00:04:58,384
Lowering it later costs nothing. And never
let severity depend on who caused the

70
00:04:58,424 --> 00:04:58,907
incident.

71
00:04:59,247 --> 00:05:05,770
Quick quiz on the first rule. A production
branch was rewritten, and nothing

72
00:05:05,810 --> 00:05:10,432
shipped. A, SEV 4. B, SEV 3.
Or C, SEV 2? Your answer?

73
00:05:15,272 --> 00:05:20,405
C. The first rule is the one people resist.
The textbook's example is exactly this,

74
00:05:20,445 --> 00:05:25,516
a rewritten production branch that shipped
nothing and is still SEV 2, because the

75
00:05:25,556 --> 00:05:29,817
only thing between the rewritten
branch and production was one check.

76
00:05:30,157 --> 00:05:34,808
The ten incidents. Each is a directory
under incidents/ with three files.

77
00:05:35,148 --> 00:05:38,841
The report is incomplete and
partly wrong, as real reports are.

78
00:05:38,881 --> 00:05:41,507
The generator is the
answer and is read last.

79
00:05:41,847 --> 00:05:46,776
The ten-part format. The textbook uses the
same ten headings for every incident, and

80
00:05:46,816 --> 00:05:51,449
so should your notes: symptoms, evidence,
hypotheses, diagnostic commands, root

81
00:05:51,489 --> 00:05:55,827
cause, safe recovery, verification,
prevention, communication, postmortem.

82
00:05:55,867 --> 00:06:00,204
A postmortem is the written record of
how the system allowed the incident.

83
00:06:00,544 --> 00:06:05,259
Why the solution comes last. The value of
a drill is the diagnosis you make without

84
00:06:05,299 --> 00:06:08,868
help. Once you've read the cause,
that drill can't be repeated.

85
00:06:08,908 --> 00:06:11,389
A diagnosis you've read
can't be made again.

86
00:06:13,229 --> 00:06:17,308
The textbook's analogy is a ward
doctor with an unstable patient.

87
00:06:17,348 --> 00:06:22,442
The doctor follows a fixed sequence:
stabilise, record, diagnose, treat, confirm,

88
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:27,448
hand over. The sequence exists because
under pressure people skip to treatment.

89
00:06:27,788 --> 00:06:31,016
The analogy breaks in two
places, both in your favor.

90
00:06:31,056 --> 00:06:36,010
A repository can be copied in seconds, so
a treatment can be rehearsed on a copy.

91
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:41,189
And Git almost never destroys committed work
by itself, so "stabilise" usually means

92
00:06:41,229 --> 00:06:43,655
stopping people, not stopping a process.

93
00:06:43,995 --> 00:06:47,643
That second point changes what
the first minute looks like.

94
00:06:47,683 --> 00:06:52,831
In most Git incidents the first act is a
sentence, not a command: "Nobody pushes to

95
00:06:52,871 --> 00:06:58,020
or resets this branch until I say so." The
textbook's production example is exactly

96
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:02,708
that: an ML platform team deploys an
inference service from a branch called

97
00:07:02,748 --> 00:07:07,521
production, the deploy job refuses to
run, and stage 1 is one sentence in the

98
00:07:07,561 --> 00:07:08,021
channel.

99
00:07:09,861 --> 00:07:15,022
Look at "has not fetched yet". A clone
that hasn't fetched is a witness: it still

100
00:07:15,062 --> 00:07:18,938
holds the server's old state.
Look at "no reflog of its own".

101
00:07:18,978 --> 00:07:23,624
And follow the long arrow at the bottom:
communication starts at stage 1.

102
00:07:25,464 --> 00:07:28,915
How to run one. All commands
are typed in the course root.

103
00:07:29,255 --> 00:07:31,349
Every sandbox has the same layout.

104
00:07:31,689 --> 00:07:37,144
You're allowed to look into and work in
every clone, the way you would sit down at a

105
00:07:37,184 --> 00:07:42,509
colleague's machine during an incident.
git -C ../ravi <command> runs a command in

106
00:07:42,549 --> 00:07:47,744
Ravi's clone without leaving yours. You're
also the administrator of server.git.

107
00:07:47,784 --> 00:07:53,109
Two read-only commands to orient yourself
in any sandbox are git ls-remote origin,

108
00:07:53,149 --> 00:07:56,838
for what the server holds, and
git branch, in each clone.

109
00:07:57,178 --> 00:08:02,343
The generator runs with the fixed lab clock,
so the commit IDs in your sandbox equal

110
00:08:02,383 --> 00:08:05,502
the IDs printed in the
solutions and in Chapter 30.

111
00:08:05,542 --> 00:08:10,335
Commits that you create in the lab shell
use the real clock and get other IDs.

112
00:08:10,375 --> 00:08:14,610
Running the generator again deletes
the sandbox and builds it afresh.

113
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:19,691
Do that whenever a repair went wrong: an
incident sandbox is the one place where a

114
00:08:19,731 --> 00:08:21,798
destructive mistake costs nothing.

115
00:08:22,138 --> 00:08:26,853
How to solve one. Seven steps, from the
README. Read the symptoms and write the

116
00:08:26,893 --> 00:08:31,608
symptom in one sentence without interpretation.
Collect evidence with read-only

117
00:08:31,648 --> 00:08:36,544
commands only, in each clone that matters.
Write at least three hypotheses and the

118
00:08:36,584 --> 00:08:40,035
command that tells them apart.
Preserve before you change.

119
00:08:40,075 --> 00:08:44,129
Choose the fix that destroys least
and run it one command at a time.

120
00:08:44,169 --> 00:08:48,944
Verify with the commands that showed the
problem. And write the four lines a CTO

121
00:08:48,984 --> 00:08:52,014
needs. There are the four
answers from the opening.

122
00:08:52,354 --> 00:08:53,121
How to check.

123
00:08:53,461 --> 00:08:58,478
The check prints one line per condition
and ends with PASS, exit status 0, or NOT

124
00:08:58,518 --> 00:09:03,409
YET, exit status 1. A check verifies the
state you were asked to reach, not the

125
00:09:03,449 --> 00:09:08,652
route you took. It can't verify the parts
of an incident that aren't Git state, such

126
00:09:08,692 --> 00:09:11,461
as rotating a credential
or telling the team.

127
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:16,870
The symptom is the report: Ravi's git log
no longer lists the commits he made on

128
00:09:16,910 --> 00:09:21,596
Friday, and he thinks git pull ate
them. That's what a colleague believes.

129
00:09:21,636 --> 00:09:22,937
It isn't a diagnosis.

130
00:09:23,277 --> 00:09:28,005
A failing check has this shape. The script
prints one line per condition, each

131
00:09:28,045 --> 00:09:33,139
beginning with ok or FAIL, and it ends with
NOT YET, the number of failed checks and

132
00:09:33,179 --> 00:09:38,151
exit status 1. Each FAIL line names something
the recovery was supposed to keep or

133
00:09:38,191 --> 00:09:38,640
restore.

134
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:43,785
Stop here, with one prediction for your
notes. Which row of today's evidence table

135
00:09:43,825 --> 00:09:47,271
will matter most for Ravi's
missing commits? Write it down.

136
00:09:49,611 --> 00:09:54,313
I won't confirm it. The repository will.
Don't run the replay of Incident 1 and

137
00:09:54,353 --> 00:09:59,355
don't open its solution. Generate the incident
and find out from the repository what

138
00:09:59,395 --> 00:10:02,536
happened. That's your exercise
before the next video.

139
00:10:02,876 --> 00:10:06,356
Two things differ from real
life, and the README says so.

140
00:10:06,396 --> 00:10:11,481
The clock, as explained. And the server: a
bare repository has no Activity view, no

141
00:10:11,521 --> 00:10:16,297
pull requests, no rulesets and no Support
desk, all of which belong to GitHub.

142
00:10:16,337 --> 00:10:21,299
Where an incident depends on one of those,
the solution says what the GitHub-side

143
00:10:21,339 --> 00:10:24,201
step is, described from
GitHub's documentation.

144
00:10:26,041 --> 00:10:27,689
Five mistakes to watch for.

145
00:10:28,029 --> 00:10:32,865
Skipping to recovery. Root cause: under time
pressure the destructive shortcut looks

146
00:10:32,905 --> 00:10:37,219
like progress, and the loop exists to
delay it until the state is known and

147
00:10:37,259 --> 00:10:37,800
preserved.

148
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:41,169
Waiting for the root cause
before the first message.

149
00:10:41,209 --> 00:10:46,067
Root cause: communication is treated as the
end of the loop, when the team needs to

150
00:10:46,107 --> 00:10:48,487
know at once which branch to leave alone.

151
00:10:48,827 --> 00:10:53,452
Rating severity by what happened to happen.
Root cause: the window is ignored; the

152
00:10:53,492 --> 00:10:57,946
level depends on what could have reached
users or outsiders while the state was

153
00:10:57,986 --> 00:10:58,288
wrong.

154
00:10:58,628 --> 00:11:04,131
Letting colleagues "clean up" their clones.
Root cause: a server keeps no reflog, so

155
00:11:04,171 --> 00:11:09,609
the history of a server-side branch exists
only in the clones, and a pruning fetch,

156
00:11:09,649 --> 00:11:11,852
a git gc or a re-clone deletes it.

157
00:11:12,192 --> 00:11:17,186
Reading the generator or the solution first.
Root cause: the drill trains diagnosis

158
00:11:17,226 --> 00:11:22,280
from an incomplete report, and a cause that
has been read cannot be diagnosed again.

159
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:29,085
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
deploys an inference service from the

160
00:11:29,125 --> 00:11:34,347
branch production. On a Tuesday the deploy
job refuses to run, with a message that

161
00:11:34,387 --> 00:11:37,684
the deployed commit isn't
an ancestor of the branch.

162
00:11:37,724 --> 00:11:41,470
In plain words, the branch's
history no longer contains it.

163
00:11:41,810 --> 00:11:46,684
The engineer on call does three things in
the first five minutes, and none of them

164
00:11:46,724 --> 00:11:51,359
is a repair. Stage 1: one sentence in
the channel, "Nobody pushes to or resets

165
00:11:51,399 --> 00:11:56,332
production until I say so." Stage 2: she
asks two colleagues not to fetch, prune or

166
00:11:56,372 --> 00:12:01,186
re-clone, because their clones are the
witnesses, and she anchors the commits she

167
00:12:01,226 --> 00:12:04,183
can already name, giving
each a branch of its own.

168
00:12:04,523 --> 00:12:08,218
Stage 6, early: a first status
to the lead, with the level.

169
00:12:08,258 --> 00:12:13,156
She rates it SEV 2 although nothing was
deployed, and says why: the only thing

170
00:12:13,196 --> 00:12:16,829
between the rewritten branch
and production was one check.

171
00:12:17,169 --> 00:12:21,777
The diagnosis, the recovery and the
verification come after that, in that order.

172
00:12:21,817 --> 00:12:26,193
The lead later says that the first
sentence was the most valuable act of the

173
00:12:26,233 --> 00:12:30,202
incident: while it stood, nobody
added a second problem to the first.

174
00:12:32,042 --> 00:12:38,102
Your turn. Generate Incident 1,
incidents/01-hard-reset, and work on it for thirty

175
00:12:38,142 --> 00:12:41,300
minutes without help
before the next video.

176
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:46,888
Before you run anything in the sandbox,
write the symptom in one sentence without

177
00:12:46,928 --> 00:12:51,915
the reporter's interpretation, and predict
which of the evidence locations of

178
00:12:51,955 --> 00:12:55,506
today's table will matter.
Keep a log of every command.

179
00:12:55,546 --> 00:12:59,553
Use the ten headings. Run check.sh
when you think you're done.

180
00:12:59,593 --> 00:13:04,254
Don't open the generator, the solution
file or Chapter 30, section 30.5.

181
00:13:04,594 --> 00:13:09,718
The challenge: generate Incident 8,
incidents/08-branch-disappeared, as well, and

182
00:13:09,758 --> 00:13:15,073
attempt it before the next video. The next
video begins with a pause point for both.

183
00:13:18,913 --> 00:13:22,399
Answer out loud. The question
has two halves and a constraint.

184
00:13:22,439 --> 00:13:26,722
For the rating, a strong answer states
the rule it applied and the fact that

185
00:13:26,762 --> 00:13:31,386
satisfies it: what stood between the wrong
state and production during the window.

186
00:13:31,426 --> 00:13:36,050
For the root cause, two sentences, in words
a non-specialist can follow, that name

187
00:13:36,090 --> 00:13:40,771
the layer, so that the listener knows whether
the fix belongs in a person's habits,

188
00:13:40,811 --> 00:13:43,558
in a client setting or in
a rule on the platform.

189
00:13:43,898 --> 00:13:48,375
Practise the two sentences until they
contain no Git term that needs explaining.

190
00:13:48,415 --> 00:13:52,666
The constraint is the point: an answer
that takes five sentences hasn't been

191
00:13:52,706 --> 00:13:53,174
prepared.

192
00:13:55,014 --> 00:13:55,941
Let's land this.

193
00:13:56,281 --> 00:14:00,583
An incident is a problem in shared state
that others depend on while you are still

194
00:14:00,623 --> 00:14:01,800
working out what it is.

195
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:07,578
The loop: stabilise, preserve, diagnose,
recover, verify, communicate, prevent;

196
00:14:07,618 --> 00:14:13,334
stages 2 and 3 only add refs or read state,
and the first message goes out at stage

197
00:14:13,374 --> 00:14:13,472
1.

198
00:14:13,812 --> 00:14:19,150
A server keeps no reflog, so colleagues'
clones are evidence and must not be pruned,

199
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:20,623
collected or re-cloned.

200
00:14:20,963 --> 00:14:25,365
Severity is rated on what could have happened
in the window, is raised at once when

201
00:14:25,405 --> 00:14:28,415
a secret is involved, and never
depends on who caused it.

202
00:14:28,755 --> 00:14:33,406
Each incident has a report, a generator and
a check; the generator and the solution

203
00:14:33,446 --> 00:14:34,932
are read after the attempt.

204
00:14:36,772 --> 00:14:42,413
Read sections 30.1 to 30.4. Then do the two
attempts: Incident 1 for thirty minutes,

205
00:14:42,453 --> 00:14:47,756
and Incident 8 if you take the challenge.
Bring your command logs and your four

206
00:14:47,796 --> 00:14:50,190
lines for the CTO to the next video.

207
00:14:50,530 --> 00:14:54,640
You now know the loop, the scale, and
how to build and check an incident.

208
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:58,904
Make your attempt before you watch on.
Next time: the debrief of your first

209
00:14:58,944 --> 00:15:02,087
incident, and a branch that
appears to have disappeared.

210
00:15:02,127 --> 00:15:05,043
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

211
00:15:05,083 --> 00:15:06,408
See you in the next one.
